model_response:
 It seems that there are two issues in the code you provided:

1. In the first block of code, you are trying to complete the `GeckoResult` with a `PromptResponse` object, but the `PromptResponse` object is not accessible from within the `GeckoResult` object. To fix this, you need to create a separate method that returns the `PromptResponse` object and call that method from within the `GeckoResult` completion block.
2. In the second block of code, you are trying to call the `thenAccept` method on the `result` object, but the `result` object is not accessible from within the lambda expression. To fix this, you need to create a separate variable to store the `result` object and pass it as an argument to the lambda expression.

Here's an example of how you can fix these issues:
```java
@Override
public GeckoResult<PromptResponse> onFilePrompt(GeckoSession session, FilePrompt prompt) {
    // Your existing code to start the file chooser intent
    Intent intent = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_GET_CONTENT);
    intent.setType("*/*");
    intent.addCategory(Intent.CATEGORY_OPENABLE);
    currentFilePrompt = prompt;
    filePromptFuture = new CompletableFuture<>();
    startActivityForResult(intent, FILE_UPLOAD_REQUEST_CODE);

    // Create a new GeckoResult
    GeckoResult<PromptResponse> geckoResult = new GeckoResult<>();

    // When the CompletableFuture is completed, complete the GeckoResult
    filePromptFuture.whenComplete((result, throwable) -> {
        if (throwable != null) {
            // Complete the GeckoResult exceptionally if there is a throwable
            geckoResult.completeExceptionally(throwable);
        } else {
            // Otherwise, complete it with the result
            PromptResponse result = getPromptResponse(result);
            geckoResult.complete(result);
        }
    });

    return geckoResult;
}

private PromptResponse getPromptResponse(CompletableFuture<PromptResponse> result) {
    // Your code to get the PromptResponse object from the CompletableFuture
    result.thenAccept(response -> {
        // Call the lambda expression with the PromptResponse object
        geckoResult.complete((PromptResponse) response);
    });
    return null;
}
```
Note that you will need to replace the `geckoResult` variable with a variable that is accessible from within the `getPromptResponse` method.

 Adobe JavaScript can be used to force a textfield to display columns by using the following code:
```javascript
function showColumns(textField) {
  var columns = textField.value.split('\n').map(function(line) {
    return line.split(' ');
  });
  textField.value = columns.join('\n');
}
```
This function takes in a textfield object as an argument and splits the value of the textfield into an array of arrays, where each inner array represents a line of text. The function then joins the arrays back together into a single string with line breaks between each line. This will cause the textfield to display columns of text.

You can then call this function on the textfield object whenever you want to display columns of text. For example:
```javascript
var textField = document.getElementById('myTextField');
showColumns(textField);
```
This will call the `showColumns` function on the textfield with an id of "myTextField".

