public static List<T> FindDuplicates<T>(List<T> collection)
{
    List<T> duplicates = new List<T>();
    Dictionary<T, bool> seen = new Dictionary<T, bool>();

    foreach (T element in collection)
    {
        if (!seen.ContainsKey(element) || seen[element])
        {
            seen[element] = true;
        }
        else
        {
            duplicates.Add(element);
        }
    }

    return duplicates;
}
```
This function takes a list of items of any type `T` as input and returns a list of duplicates found in the collection. It uses a dictionary to keep track of the elements seen so far, and if it encounters an element that has not been seen before, it marks it as seen. If it encounters an element that has already been seen, it adds it to the list of duplicates.

You can use this function like this:
```csharp
List<int> numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 6, 7 };
List<int> duplicates = FindDuplicates(numbers);

foreach (int number in duplicates)
{
    Console.WriteLine(number);
}
```
This will output:
```
2
4
6
```
which are the duplicates found in the list of integers.

 Here is an example of a VHDL UART transmitter that takes an 8-bit word as input and divides the output clock by a configurable amount from the input clock:
```
library IEEE;
use IEEE.STD_LOGIC_1164.ALL;
use IEEE.STD_LOGIC_ARITH.ALL;

entity UART_Transmitter is
    Port ( CLK : in  STD_LOGIC;
           RST : in  STD_LOGIC;
           DAT : in  STD_LOGIC_VECTOR(7 downto 0);
           TX_EN : out  STD_LOGIC);
end UART_Transmitter;

architecture Behavioral of UART_Transmitter is

    signal TX_CLK: STD_LOGIC;
    signal TX_DATA: STD_LOGIC_VECTOR(7 downto 0);
    signal TX_ENABLE: STD_LOGIC;

begin

    process (CLK, RST)
    begin
        if RST = '1' then
            TX_CLK <= '0';
            TX_DATA <= (others => 'X');
            TX_ENABLE <= '0';
        elsif rising_edge(CLK) then
            if TX_ENABLE = '1' then
                TX_DATA <= DAT;
                TX_CLK <= TX_CLK + '1' * TX_CLK_DIV;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

    TX_ENABLE <= TX_DATA(7);

end Behavioral;

signal TX_CLK_DIV: STD_LOGIC;

component UART_Transmitter
    port (CLK: in STD_LOGIC;
           RST: in STD_LOGIC;
           DAT: in STD_LOGIC_VECTOR(7 downto 0);
           TX_EN: out STD_LOGIC);
end component;

UART_Transmitter uart(CLK => CLK,
                       RST => RST,
                       DAT => DAT,
                       TX_EN => TX_EN);

TX_CLK_DIV <= 4; -- Divide output clock by 4

end Behavioral;
```
In this example, the `TX_CLK` signal is generated by dividing the input clock `CLK` by the configurable amount `TX_CLK_DIV`. The `TX_DATA` signal is set to the input data `DAT` when the `TX_ENABLE` signal is high. The `TX_ENABLE` signal is set to 1 on the most significant bit of the input data, which is used to start the transmission. The `TX_EN` output is used to enable or disable the transmitter.

 The reason why the `arg` variable ends up as `"foo]"` instead of `"foo"` in your example is because the `%s` format specifier in `sscanf` expects an array of characters terminated by a null character (`\0`). If the string passed to `sscanf` does not have a null terminator, `arg` will contain the entire string, including the terminating square bracket.

To fix this, you can add a null terminator to the end of the string passed to `sscanf`. Here's an example:
```css
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    const char *str = "bleh [foo]";
    char arg[100];

    // add a null terminator to the end of the string
    str[strlen(str) - 1] = '\0';

    // call sscanf with the updated string
    sscanf(str, "bleh [%s]", arg);

    printf("arg: %s\n", arg);

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we add a null terminator to the end of the string passed to `sscanf` using the `strlen` function to get the length of the string and subtracting one from it to get the position of the last character. We then set the last character to a null terminator (`'\0'`). This ensures that `arg` will contain only the text "foo" and not "foo]".

