A reverse proxy is a server that sits between a client and a server, forwarding requests from the client to the server and returning the response back to the client. In this case, you want to use Nginx as a reverse proxy to forward requests to a Flask application running in a subdirectory.

Here are the basic steps you can follow:

1. Install Nginx on your server.
2. Create a new Nginx configuration file, for example /etc/nginx/sites-available/reverse-proxy.conf, and add the following content:
```bash
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;
    root /var/www/flask-app;
    index index.html index.htm index.htm;

    location /flask {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:5000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```
3. Enable the configuration file by running the following command:
```bash
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/reverse-proxy.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
```
4. Restart Nginx to apply the changes:
```
sudo systemctl restart nginx
```
In this configuration, Nginx is listening on port 80 and forwarding requests to the Flask application running on http://localhost:5000. The /flask location block is used to specify the URL path to forward requests to.

Note that this is a basic configuration and you may need to adjust it to fit your specific needs. Additionally, you may need to configure your Flask application to use the correct URL scheme and path prefix for the Nginx reverse proxy.

