 You can use the `useEffect` hook along with the `useRef` hook to achieve this. Here's how you can do it:
```
import React, { useState, useEffect, useRef } from 'react';

function MyComponent() {
  const elementRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!elementRef.current) {
      return;
    }

    const handleScroll = () => {
      const { bottom } = elementRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
      if (bottom < window.innerHeight) {
        elementRef.current.scrollIntoView();
      }
    };

    window.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);

    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div ref={elementRef}>
      <!-- Your content here -->
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, we use the `useRef` hook to create a reference to the element that we want to scroll into view. Then, we use the `useEffect` hook to listen for scroll events and check if the bottom of the element is not visible in the window. If it is not visible, we call the `scrollIntoView` method on the element to scroll it into view. We also add a cleanup function to remove the scroll event listener when the component unmounts.

